# Feature: Download Recipe as a PDF

**Feature ID:** 6
**Branch pattern:** `feature-6-download-recipe-pdf`
**Status:** Ready
**Created:** 2026-17-09
**Input:** Signed in user can download their recipes into a viewable PDF from the recipe menu 
**Related:** `features/reference/api.md`, `features/reference/data-model.md`, `features/reference/behavior.md`
**Depends on** `feature-1-account-management`, `feature-2-ingredients-management`, `feature-3-recipe-management`, `feature/4-recipe-ingredient-management`, `feature-5-published-recipe-mangement`


---

## User Stories


### US-6.1: Download a recipe as a PDF

**As a** signed-in user with an existing recipe
**I want to** download my recipe as a PDF
**So that** I can view my recipe as a PDF on my local machine

**Priority:** P1
**Independent test:** Click download, have the file menu pop up, choose a file name, choose a location, click save and PDF exists in local file explorer
**Acceptance scenarios:** see ### US-6.1 under Acceptance Criteria


### US-6.2: Open a downloaded recipe PDF

**As a** user with a downloaded recipe
**I want to** open my recipe PDF
**So that** I can view my recipe steps, ingredients in an organized view

**Priority:** P2
**Independent test:** Open the file explorer, open the recipe PDF and view the recipe's steps and ingredients
**Acceptance scenarios:** see ### US-6.2 under Acceptance Criteria


## Requirements

### Functional Requirements

- **FR-001**: Users MUST have at least one recipe to be able to download it.
- **FR-002**: User MUST be signed in a valid session to click download.
- **FR-003**: Downloading a recipe MUST open up the users file explorer to select location and file name.
- **FR-004**: A downloaded recipe MUST have the `oc_logo.png` in the upper left hand corner of the PDF.
- **FR-005**: The generated PDF MUST include the recipe **name**, an **Ingredients** list (quantity, unit, and name), and a **Steps** table (step number, instruction, and ingredients used on that step) so the file is readable after save.
- **FR-006**: The download control MUST be the PDF icon (`mdi-file-pdf-box`) on the recipe card on the recipes page (`recipes` / `/recipes`). It MUST be shown only when `user` is in `localStorage`. Clicking it MUST generate the PDF for that recipe and MUST NOT navigate away from the recipes page.
- **FR-007**: PDF generation MUST use that recipe’s stored ingredients and steps (Feature 4 services). The app MUST NOT persist PDF files on the server and MUST NOT invent a PDF download API in this feature.



## Assumptions

- Recipe create/edit/delete is Feature 3. Recipe steps and recipe-ingredient rows are Feature 4. This feature only exports an existing recipe to a PDF.
- Session, `localStorage` key `user`, and route guards are Feature 1. Download requires a signed-in user with a valid session.
- The PDF is generated in the browser from the recipe already shown on the recipes page. The save dialog MAY start with a default file name; the user still chooses the final name and location (**FR-003**).
- The logo asset is `oc_logo.png` (same brand mark as the MenuBar).
- Guests who view published recipes (Feature 1 / Feature 5) cannot download a PDF.
- Ingredient CRUD, recipe CRUD, step/ingredient editing, and the publish toggle stay Features 2–5.



## Edge Cases

- No recipes for the signed-in user → there is no recipe card, so download is not available.
- Guest or signed-out user on the recipes page → the PDF download control is hidden.
- Recipe with no ingredients and/or no steps → PDF still downloads; those sections are empty rather than blocking save.
- User cancels the save / file dialog → no file is written; the recipe is unchanged.
- Missing or expired session → the PDF icon is hidden (**FR-002**, **FR-006**). Feature 4 list `GET`s used to fill the PDF do not require a token in the running app; this feature does not add a PDF endpoint that returns `401`.



## Success Criteria

- **SC-001**: Every Gherkin scenario in this feature has at least one automated test before merge.
- **SC-002**: A signed-in user with an existing recipe can click download, choose a name and location, and find the PDF in their file explorer in one manual pass.
- **SC-003**: `npm test` passes with coverage for the PDF download control and generated content mapped to US-6.1 and US-6.2.
- **SC-004**: Opening the saved PDF shows the recipe name, ingredients, and steps in an organized layout, with `oc_logo.png` in the upper left.

